  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "To predict an unobserved variable $X$ given an observed variable $Y$ from a joint distribution $P(X, Y, Z)$, compute the conditional distribution $P(X | Y) = \\frac{P(X, Y)}{P(Y)}$",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:12:44\u201300:13:20] So actually, these are two basic rules of working with probabilities. And it appears that if we know both rules, we can easily derive different predictions on some variables given some other variables. So let's consider the following situation. We have a joint distribution over three variables, field X, Y, and Z. Suppose that we observe Y, and we would like to predict what values X can take."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The numerator $P(X, Y)$ can be obtained by integrating the joint distribution $P(X, Y, Z)$ over $Z$",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:14:25\u201300:14:59] So this is po x y over po y. And now we can express both numerator and denominator using the sum rule. So we can obtain joint distribution on x y from joint distribution on x y z simply by integrating our z."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The denominator $P(Y)$ can be obtained by integrating the joint distribution $P(X, Y, Z)$ over $Z$ and $X$",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:14:25\u201300:14:59] And in denominator we take joint distribution and integrate our z and x."
    },
    {
      "claim": "A probabilistic model is a joint distribution over the whole set of variables that arise in the problem",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:14:59\u201300:15:28] If we have access to the joint distribution, we will call probabilistic model. So a probabilistic model is simply a joint distribution over the whole set of variables that arise in our problem."
    },
    {
      "claim": "Given a joint distribution over $X$, $Y$, $Z$, observing $Y$ allows prediction of $X$ using $P(X | Y) = \\frac{P(X, Y)}{P(Y)}$",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:13:38\u201300:14:25] So we're interested in computing P of x given y. So y is observed, x is a variable of interest, and we don't care about z."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The sum rule (integration) is used to derive conditional distributions from joint distributions",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:14:25\u201300:14:59] So we can express both numerator and denominator using the sum rule."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The conditional distribution $P(X | Y)$ can be computed if we have access to the joint distribution $P(X, Y, Z)$",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:14:59\u201300:15:28] If we have access to the joint distribution, we can easily, at least in theory, we can easily make arbitrary predictions."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The joint distribution $P(X, Y, Z)$ is sufficient for computing conditional distributions $P(X | Y)$",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:14:25\u201300:14:59] So we can obtain joint distribution on x y from joint distribution on x y z simply by integrating our z."
    }
  ],
